1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,571
JAKE: Now that we've done all the research into bike-sharing schemes in cities around the world,

2
00:00:06,571 --> 00:00:11,500
we need to think about how we're going to organise our report.

3
00:00:11,500 --> 00:00:15,100
AMY: Right. I think we should start by talking about the benefits.

4
00:00:15,100 --> 00:00:16,705
I mean it's great that

5
00:00:16,705 --> 00:00:19,080
so many cities have introduced these schemes

6
00:00:19,080 --> 00:00:22,803
where anyone can pick up a bike from dozens of different locations

7
00:00:22,803 --> 00:00:25,050
and hire it for a few hours.

8
00:00:25,050 --> 00:00:28,030
It makes riding a bike very convenient for people.

9
00:00:28,030 --> 00:00:31,160
JAKE: Yes, but the costs can add up

10
00:00:31,160 --> 00:00:34,070
and that puts people on low incomes off in some places.

11
00:00:34,070 --> 00:00:40,432
AMY: I suppose so, but if it means more people in general are cycling rather than driving,

12
00:00:40,432 --> 00:00:45,340
then because they're increasing the amount of physical activity they do

13
00:00:45,340 --> 00:00:46,420
it's good for their health.

14
00:00:46,420 --> 00:00:49,820
JAKE: OK. But isn't that of less importance?

15
00:00:49,820 --> 00:00:57,250
I mean, doesn't the impact of reduced emissions on air pollution have a more significant effect on people's health?

16
00:00:57,250 --> 00:01:02,440
AMY: Certainly, in some cities bike-sharing has made a big contribution to that.

17
00:01:02,440 --> 00:01:06,480
And also helped to cut the number of cars on the road significantly.

18
00:01:06,480 --> 00:01:07,920
JAKE: Which is the main point.

19
00:01:07,920 --> 00:01:12,837
AMY: Exactly. But I'd say it's had less of an impact on noise pollution

20
00:01:12,837 --> 00:01:16,350
because there are still loads of buses and lorries around.

21
00:01:16,350 --> 00:01:17,290
JAKE: Right.

22
00:01:17,290 --> 00:01:21,050
AMY: Shall we quickly discuss the recommendations we're going to make?

23
00:01:21,050 --> 00:01:23,760
JAKE: In order to ensure bike-sharing schemes are successful?

24
00:01:23,760 --> 00:01:24,630
AMY: Yes.

25
00:01:24,630 --> 00:01:31,792
JAKE: OK. Well, while I think it's nice to have really state-of-the-art bikes with things like GPS,

26
00:01:31,792 --> 00:01:34,180
I wouldn't say they're absolutely necessary.

27
00:01:34,180 --> 00:01:37,667
AMY: But some technical things are really important -

28
00:01:37,667 --> 00:01:43,770
like a fully functional app - so people can make payments and book bikes easily.

29
00:01:43,770 --> 00:01:47,440
Places which haven't invested in that have really struggled.

30
00:01:47,440 --> 00:01:53,194
JAKE: Good point... Some people say there shouldn't be competing companies offering separate bike-sharing schemes,

31
00:01:53,194 --> 00:01:55,496
but in some really big cities,

32
00:01:55,496 --> 00:02:01,250
competition's beneficial and anyway one company might not be able to manage the whole thing.

33
00:02:01,250 --> 00:02:06,050
AMY: Right. Deciding how much to invest is a big question.

34
00:02:06,050 --> 00:02:15,103
Cities which have opened loads of new bike lanes at the same time as introducing bike-sharing schemes have generally been more successful -

35
00:02:15,103 --> 00:02:19,630
but there are examples of successful schemes where this hasn't happened...

36
00:02:19,630 --> 00:02:23,310
What does matter though - is having a big publicity campaign.

37
00:02:23,310 --> 00:02:29,620
JAKE: Definitely. If people don't know how to use the scheme or don't understand its benefits, they won't use it.

38
00:02:29,620 --> 00:02:37,210
People need a lot of persuasion to stop using their cars.

39
00:02:37,210 --> 00:02:40,050
AMY: Shall we look at some examples now?

40
00:02:40,050 --> 00:02:43,420
And say what we think is good or bad about them.

41
00:02:43,420 --> 00:02:44,966
JAKE: I suppose we should start

42
00:02:44,966 --> 00:02:48,830
with Amsterdam as this was one of the first cities to have a bike-sharing scheme.

43
00:02:48,830 --> 00:02:53,400
AMY: Yes. There was already a strong culture of cycling here.

44
00:02:53,400 --> 00:02:57,425
In a way it's strange that there was such a demand for bike-sharing

45
00:02:57,425 --> 00:03:01,140
because you'd have thought most people would have used their own bikes.

46
00:03:01,140 --> 00:03:03,400
JAKE: And yet it's one of the best-used schemes...

47
00:03:03,400 --> 00:03:07,130
Dublin's an interesting example of a success story.

48
00:03:07,130 --> 00:03:10,850
AMY: It must be because the public transport system's quite limited.

49
00:03:10,850 --> 00:03:13,140
JAKE: Not really - there's no underground

50
00:03:13,180 --> 00:03:15,380
but there are trams and a good bus network.

51
00:03:15,380 --> 00:03:18,580
I'd say price has a lot to do with it.

52
00:03:18,580 --> 00:03:21,380
It's one of the cheapest schemes in Europe to join.

53
00:03:21,380 --> 00:03:27,390
AMY: But the buses are really slow - anyway the weather certainly can't be a factor!

54
00:03:27,390 --> 00:03:31,730
JAKE: No - definitely not. The London scheme's been quite successful.

55
00:03:31,730 --> 00:03:34,880
AMY: Yes - it's been a really good thing for the city.

56
00:03:34,880 --> 00:03:38,940
The bikes are popular and the whole system is well maintained

57
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:41,380
but it isn't expanding quickly enough.

58
00:03:41,380 --> 00:03:46,860
JAKE: Basically, not enough's been spent on increasing the number of cycle lanes. Hopefully that'll change.

59
00:03:46,860 --> 00:03:51,380
AMY: Yes. Now what about outside Europe?

60
00:03:51,380 --> 00:03:55,350
JAKE: Well bike-sharing schemes have taken off in places like Buenos Aires.

61
00:03:55,350 --> 00:04:01,270
AMY: Mmm. They built a huge network of cycle lanes to support the introduction of the scheme there, didn't they?

62
00:04:01,270 --> 00:04:06,070
It attracted huge numbers of cyclists where previously there were hardly any.

63
00:04:06,070 --> 00:04:07,880
JAKE: An example of good planning.

64
00:04:07,880 --> 00:04:13,440
AMY: Absolutely. New York is a good example of how not to introduce a scheme.

65
00:04:13,440 --> 00:04:19,030
When they launched it, it was more than ten times the price of most other schemes.

66
00:04:19,030 --> 00:04:21,290
JAKE: More than it costs to take a taxi.

67
00:04:21,290 --> 00:04:26,070
Crazy. I think the organisers lacked vision and ambition there.

68
00:04:26,070 --> 00:04:30,400
AMY: I think so too. Sydney would be a good example to use.

69
00:04:30,400 --> 00:04:33,450
I would have expected it to have grown pretty quickly here.

70
00:04:33,450 --> 00:04:39,640
JAKE: Yes. I can't quite work out why it hasn't been an instant success like some of the others.

71
00:04:39,640 --> 00:04:41,230
It's a shame really.

72
00:04:41,230 --> 00:04:46,000
AMY: I know. OK so now we've thought about...
